 { The majority of the ideas centering around pawns have already been covered in the previous chapter, so we will not focus on these here. Instead, the main positional topic has to do with avoiding placing your pawns on the wrong color in certain types of endgames. We'll cover a few examples of these soon. Before we do this, let's show a couple of positions to illustrate and recap some of the concepts previously introduced. } 1... { -KEY- } Rg4 $1 { Black uses the pseudo outpost on g4 to activate the rook, benefiting from the fact that f3 would weaken the g3 pawn. } 2. Ke3 b5 { Looking to launch a minority attack, discussed in the chapter on 'Pawn Play'. } 3. Kf3 a5 4. Rc1 Rc8 { Occupying the semi-open file. } 5. Rhd1 f5 $1 { Trying to undouble the pawns by playing the pawn break with ...f4, which would at the same time damage White's kingside pawn chain. } 6. a3 a4 { Fixing the backward pawn on b2. } 7. Kg2 f4 $1 8. Rd3 f6 { Activating the central pawn majority. } 9. Kh3 e5 10. dxe5+ fxe5 11. Re1 $2 ( { Under pressure, White makes a significant mistake. Much more resilient was } 11. Rcd1 { . } ) 
fxg3 12. fxg3 e4 $1 { The protected passed pawn is very strong here, Black is already winning. } 13. Rde3 Rf8 $1 { Occupying the open file and threatening to invade along the seventh rank. } 14. R1e2 Rg7 15. Re1 Rgf7 16. R1e2 Ke5 17. Kg2 { White is largely shuffling the pieces around, as they lack an active plan. Black's space advantage is suffocating them across all flanks. } Rf3 18. Rxf3 Rxf3 19. Re1 d4 $19 { Black will win due to a combination of the strong passed pawn, and the possibility to target White's b2 pawn once lines of attack are opened up. We can see from this lightly annotated sequence that a lot of the positional concepts that you will seek to use in the endgame have already been covered in previous chapters. This is true both in the case of general concepts (space, outposts, open files) as well as pawn play concepts (pawn breaks, minority attack, pawn majorities, passed pawns, etc). } 
