 { In this example, White can use their knowledge of strong pawns (flank pawns, connected pawns) to force a winning endgame. } 1. { -KEY- } Rxa3 $1 { Initiating a series of exchanges, in order to simplify the position and force a winning endgame. } Rxa3 2. Rxa3 Bxb5 { White knows that Black has to capture the pawn, since otherwise connected passed pawns are too strong. } 3. Rb3 $1 { The bishop is struck, forcing the rooks off. } Ba6 4. Rxb7 Bxb7 { The presence of a flank pawn makes Black's defensive task close to impossible. To make matters worse, Black also has an isolated pawn on d5, an easy target for the White king. } 5. Ke3 $1 Kf8 6. h4 $1 { Fixing the pawns on light squares is helpful since Black's bishop is on a light square. } Ne8 7. Nb5 $1 { Preventing the knight from reaching a more active square by blocking d6 and c7. } Ke7 8. Kd4 { Activating the king, the topic of the final section of this chapter. } Kd7 9. Kc5 Ba6 10. Ned4 { White has excellent piece coordination, and will soon be ready to advance their a-pawn. } Bb7 11. a4 { Black has no way of fighting against White's continued advances. In this game we saw various themes, both broadly speaking and specific to pawns. In particular, White's simplifying sequence was based on an understanding of the strength of both connected pawns as well as outside (flank) passed pawns. The other helpful detail was that Black had an isolated pawn on d5 which was poorly placed on the same color as their bishop. Speaking of which, pawns on the same color as one's bishop is the central theme in the remaining examples of the section. Let's check out what is meant by this now. } 
