 { Very often you will reach a minor piece endgame with either bishop vs bishop or bishop vs knight. In such cases, it's important to be cautious when thinking about placing your pawns on the same color as your bishop. In the following game, GM Sergei Zhigalko has an equal endgame against Magnus Carlsen. However, with his next move things immediately go downhill. } 1... { -KEY- } g4 $2 ( { Waiting patiently with a move like } 1... Bf3 $11 { was enough to hold the balance. The pawn on g5 and the light-squared bishop do a great job of stopping the White king from breaking into the enemy camp on the kingside. } ) 
2. Bg2 { Suddenly, the position is already losing. } Kd7 3. Be4 h6 4. Ke3 $1 { This is the problem. Now Black is imbalanced along the dark squares, with his most central pawns (g4 and e6) both only able to control light squares. } Bd1 5. Kf4 Kc7 { In comparison, we can see that Magnus has arranged his pawns on the right squares. They coordinate with the bishop to keep the enemy king at bay. } 6. Bd3 $1 { Preparing to reroute the bishop to target the e6 pawn. } Kc6 7. Bc4 $1 Kd7 { Having just stepped forward, the king is forced to step back immediately. The other drawback of Black's strategy is revealed: White can attack the enemy pawns with his bishop, while Black cannot do likewise. } 8. Bb5+ Ke7 9. Bc6 $1 { The bishop may be zigzagging around the board, but it is not purposeless. Its next target in sight is the g4 pawn! } Be2 10. Be4 Kf7 { The most stubborn defense, taking away the g6 square. } ( { In the game Black lost quickly after } 10... Bd1 { since now White was able to play } 11. Bg6 Kd7 12. Bh5 $18 { The pawn on g4 falls. } ) 
11. Bc2 $1 { The Black bishop is now stuck on e2, since if it steps on to the f3 square then White will reply with 12.h3! } Kg7 ( 11... Bf3 { The problem here is that White can exploit the fact that the g4 pawn is the only defender of the bishop. } 12. h3 $1 h5 13. h4 $1 { 14.Kg5 will come next. } ) 
 ( { If } 11... Ke7 { then } 12. Bg6 { . } ) 
12. Bb3 { Targeting the e6 pawn. } Kf7 13. Ke3 $1 { The bishop is forced to abandon the d1-h5 diagonal. } Bb5 ( 13... Bf3 { runs into the same problem of } 14. h3 $18 ) 
14. Bd1 $1 { The attack on the g4 pawn forces Black into placing one of his remaining dark-squared pawns on a light square, making their position still more vulnerable. } h5 15. Kf4 Kg6 16. Bc2+ Kh6 { The problem is that since the h-pawn no longer controls the g5 entry point, Black's king is tied down to its defense. } 17. Bb3 $1 { Back to tormenting the e6 pawn! } Bd7 18. Ke4 $1 { With the Black king far away from the queenside, now is the perfect time to strike on the other flank. } Kg7 19. Kd4 Kf8 20. Kc5 Ke7 { The king has scrambled back just in time to protect the d6 entry point. } 21. Bc2 $1 { The painful maneuvering continues. Now the threat is 22.Bg6. } Be8 { Black's position is hanging on by a thread. The bishop on e8 is covering two critical squares: c6 and g6. In such situations, the devastating blow often comes at the hands of zugzwang. } 22. Bd3 $1 { White spots that the opponent is paralysed, and simply passes the move! } Bf7 ( 22... Kd7 { is not possible since after } 23. Bb5 Ke7 24. Bxe8 Kxe8 25. Kd6 $18 { the pawn ending is lost. } ) 
23. Kc6 { In an ironic end, it is the only dark-squared pawn on a7 that is the first to fall. Black can resign here as White's passed a-pawn will decide matters. This example was admittedly a very challenging one, with lots of maneuvering. The important thing is not so much the difficulty level but rather the ideas in action. We see that once Black played the initial mistake with 1...g4? it led to a chink in his armor that set the foundation for an eventual defeat. Slowly, White used a mix of tactics and other techniques like zugzwang to force an entry along the weakened dark squares. Black for their part was unable to generate any counterplay since White's pawns were all sitting on dark squares, making them immune from attacks by the enemy bishop. } 
