 { Here's another easier example of the same effect in action. In this case, Magnus with the White pieces was able to quickly dispatch GM Andrew Tang due to the combination of two advantages: the right color pawns, and the 2 vs 1 pawn majority on the queenside. } 1. { -KEY- } Be2 Bf7 2. Bc4 ( 2. b5 $2 { White must remain vigilant to not give up control of dark squares by pushing the pawns too soon. } exf4 $1 3. gxf4 Kc5 $11 { With the king able to step on to c5, the game is now completely equal. e.g. } 4. b6 axb6 5. axb6 Kxb6 6. Kd4 $11 { Black cannot hope to make use of their extra pawn, since the structure and king activity are both in White's favor. } ) 
Bh5 3. Ba6 { Magnus is scouting for the easy prey on f5. } Bf7 4. Bc8 exf4 ( { Keeping the central tension with } 4... Bg6 { would not have changed anything, since after } 5. fxe5 Kxe5 6. b5 Kd6 7. b6 axb6 8. axb6 $18 { While the Black king monitors the passed b-pawn, White will capture all of Black's kingside pawns! } ) 
5. gxf4 g6 { A nasty consequence of having one pawn on the wrong color is that the other pawns tend to follow suit, as they form chains to defend each other. } 6. Kd4 Bb3 7. Ba6 { Black resigned here. Let's show how the game might have played out: } Bf7 8. Bc4 Be8 9. b5 Bd7 10. b6 axb6 11. axb6 Be8 ( 11... Kc6 12. Bb5 $1 { wins on the spot. } ) 
12. Bd5 { As in the previous example, Black is in zugzwang. The bishop cannot move without allowing Bf7. } Kd7 13. Kc5 { The king pushes deeper into the position, threatening 14.Bc6+. } Kc8 14. Kd6 Kb8 15. Ke7 Bb5 16. Kf6 { The pawns are defenseless, and the White king is hungry! So far we have only looked at examples involving bishop vs bishop. However, the same often holds true when battling with bishop vs knight as in the next position. } 
