 { This game brings back painful memories for me. I was playing for Ireland at the 2018 Olympiad and after playing a rather poor game throughout, I had managed to carve my way back into an equal position. In my desire to simplify the game, I made a grave positional error. So far throughout the course, you have been challenged to find the best move on many occasions. Now that we're approaching the finishing line, let's have a light-hearted change of pace. Can you find the terrible positional move that I played?! } 38... { -KEY- } f5 $2 { Correct! This was indeed the bad move that I played! :) Miraculously, Black is not yet lost. However, now the defense becomes much more tricky. } ( 38... h6 $11 { A normal move like this would have guaranteed the draw, since there is no way for White to make progress. There is no schema that White can apply to break the fortress. There are only two vulnerabilities in the Black position: the f5 square and the b5 square. Currently Black has the f5 square under control, while if White maneuvers their knight to c3 (or a3) in order to hit the b5 pawn then Black can always step their bishop back to e8 to protect the pawn on b5. } ) 
39. gxf5+ $1 { White does not want to allow my pawn to reach the dark square on f4. } Bxf5 40. Kg3 { This is the problem. While the computer might scream that there is still a path to equality, the position has become dangerous and one more slip will be immediately fatal. } h6 $2 { The losing move. Arranging the kingside pawns on dark squares makes sense at first glance, but the problem is that they will not stay there for long. } ( 40... Be4 $1 { The only move that holds. The clever point is that after } 41. Kg4 Kf6 42. f3 { Black now has a saving resource with } Bf5 $1 { since after } 43. Nxf5 h5 44. Kxh5 Kxf5 45. Kh6 Kf6 $11 { The opposition secures a draw, in spite of Black being a pawn down. } ) 
41. h4 $1 { White goes for pawn exchanges in order to activate his king. For a third example in a row, the defending side is in zugzwang! } gxh4+ ( 41... Bg6 { Moving the bishop loses control of the g4 square. } 42. hxg5 hxg5 43. Kg4 $18 { There is no way to avoid losing either the g5 or d5 pawn. } ) 
42. Kxh4 Bg6 { Stopping Kh5 for now. } 43. Kg4 Be8 44. Kf4 Bg6 45. Ng4 $1 { The same technique as in previous examples. White targets the lone dark-squared pawn to force it to move on to a light square. } h5 { The g5 square has become an entry point for the enemy king. I could have resigned here, but struggled on for a few more moves: } 46. Ne3 Bd3 47. Kg5 Be2 48. Ng2 { The knight will jump to f4 next and grab the h5 pawn, so I resigned in Zaibi,A - Astaneh Lopez,A (2018). This concludes the section. The main takeaway is that it pays to stay alert and avoid placing your pawns on the same color as your bishop unless you have an excellent reason for doing so. While there are exceptions to any rule, this advice is pretty reliable and if you are ever unsure of the best way to arrange your pawns it is almost always best to default to the opposite color as your bishop. In this way, they will form a good team with your piece: the bishop will control one set of squares, while the pawns will protect the other set. There is only one more section left in this chapter, and indeed in the entire course! And so, it seems fitting to conclude our work by focusing on the one piece that has remained mostly silent until now - the King! } 
 *
